# Restrained autonomous orchestration — defer sequencing and chaining to the user

When driving a multi-step or multi-agent workflow, the agent must not eagerly decide how steps are sequenced, parallelized, or chained. Both the decision to run tasks in parallel (checking for shared state first) and the decision to trigger downstream steps (e.g. review after ticket work) must either follow an explicit user preference or be held for user approval. Default to the most conservative, least-surprising posture: sequential, and stop at the natural end of the requested scope.

## Procedures
- Before parallelizing any two agent tasks, check whether they touch overlapping files or shared state; if yes, serialize them.
- After completing a discrete workflow step (e.g. ticket execution), stop — do not automatically trigger the next logical step (e.g. code review, deploy, merge) unless the user explicitly requested it.
- When in doubt about scope of autonomous follow-on action, surface the decision to the user rather than acting.

## Conventions
- Treat 'next logical step' intuitions as prompts to ask, not as instructions to act.

